@scene(40)

// A sphere of water whose surface carries waves of 4D gradient noise: time is
// the noise's fourth axis, so the waves change shape as they travel, carried
// along at a velocity you set per axis. Press play to start the clock.

// The waves' velocity, in mm/s along each axis.

waterMat, waterColor = glass.water();

waveVx = 2.0;
waveVx.min = -10;
waveVx.max = 10;
waveVy = 1.0;
waveVy.min = -10;
waveVy.max = 10;
waveVz = 3.0;
waveVz.min = -10;
waveVz.max = 10;

// How fast the waves change shape as they go: the noise's cell size along
// time, in seconds. Smaller changes faster.
waveMorph = 3.0;
waveMorph.min = 0.5;
waveMorph.max = 10;

// The waves' size (the noise's cell size, in mm) and height (a multiple of
// the noise's own height, which grows with its size).
waveSize = 2.5;
waveSize.min = 1;
waveSize.max = 12;
waveHeight = 2.0;
waveHeight.min = 0;
waveHeight.max = 4;

// The noise is sampled where the waves were time seconds ago, so they travel
// at the chosen velocity. They add at most waveHeight to the field's slope, so
// the sum is divided by 1 + waveHeight to keep it a safe distance for the
// march.
drift = p - [waveVx, waveVy, waveVz] * time;
waves = waveHeight * remap(noise.gradientBump4(waveSize, waveMorph), drift);
water = (sphere(12) - waves) / (1 + waveHeight);
// Clear water shows the dark background through it. Letting 60% through
// leaves the rest to scatter the blue, and the colour tints what does pass
// over the sphere's 24 mm width.
water.material = material.withTransmission(waterMat, 0.6, 24);
water.color = [0.15, 0.45, 0.75];

return water;
